<table><tr><td align="left">



<P><STRONG>Weather News</STRONG></P>

<P><FONT SIZE="-1"><EM>Jeff Andresen, Geography</EM></FONT></P>

<BR WP="BR1"><BR WP="BR2">
<P><FONT SIZE="-1"></FONT><FONT SIZE="-1"><STRONG>2001 Drought Summary</STRONG></FONT></P>

<BR WP="BR1"><BR WP="BR2">
<P><FONT SIZE="-1">Drought and moisture stress plagued nearly all of Michigan to some extent during the 2001growing season (in some
cases continuing up to the present).  The 2001 drought was a 'classic' drought in an agricultural sense given its poor
timing.  From late April through early June, much of the state, especially western and southern sections, experienced
persistent heavy rain and ironically, some flooding (e.g. more than 10 inches of rain fell in the Grand Rapids area
during May alone, a new record total for the month).  The early wetness in these areas delayed planting of summer
crops and resulted in shallower than normal rooting systems for crops already established.  A major change in the
configuration of the jet steam across North America in mid-June led to a much drier weather pattern which persisted
from late June through mid-August.  This pattern is well-illustrated in the daily time series of observed vs. normal
precipitation accumulation and daily precipitation totals at Lansing from late May through late August in Figure 1. 
Given normal monthly precipitation totals in the state ranging from 2.8-3.6 inches, the rainfall deficit at Lansing by
mid-August was likely at least 3 inches.  Statewide, dryness and moisture stress conditions were most intense in
northern and eastern sections, with NOAA's Palmer drought index which characterizes long term, hydrological
surpluses and deficits, categorizing conditions during much of late July and August as 'moderate to severe drought'. 
In many of the worst impacted areas, precipitation deficits of 3-6 inches or more were common.  In a meteorological
sense, the drought was associated with a large, persistent upper air ridge across the Upper Midwest and warm, dry
air aloft, which  effectively inhibited convection across the state (this is the reason a series of rainfall-bearing
weather disturbances moving into the Michigan from the west rapidly dissipated as they moved into the state, with
only sprinkles or light rainfall totals). The abnormal dryness in Michigan was part of a regional pattern, stretching
from Michigan eastward through southern Ontario, sections of Ohio, Pennsylvania, New York and into New
England., and more recently, into crop production areas of the central Corn Belt region.</FONT></P>

<BR WP="BR1"><BR WP="BR2">
<P><FONT SIZE="-1">In terms of timing, the drought impacted many summer crops during moisture-sensitive growth stages and/or stages
of greatest water need, leading to moisture stress which peaked in the first half of August. Reported crop damage
was highly variable from area to area, with some spots benefitting from very timely rains and/or from soil profiles
with greater stored water availability (plant available moisture from the top 6 feet of the soil profile at field capacity
typically ranges from 3-8 inches for soils in Michigan). Recent rains from mid-August up through the present have
helped ease the dryness, but for many crops, will be too late to reverse the cumulative negative effects of the
prolonged, poorly-timed moisture stress.</FONT></P>

<BR WP="BR1"><BR WP="BR2">
<P><FONT SIZE="-1"><center><img src="./fig08-01.gif" width="518" height="646" ></center>Figure 1. </FONT></P>





